Scene 6: "Power Plays at Sublime Point"
\f1\b0 \
Sarah found Eloise on the eastern terrace, overlooking the fog-shrouded valleys that rolled away from Sublime Point. The older woman didn't turn at her approach, simply continued inspecting the elaborate knotwork pattern in the marble balustrade.\
"These carvings were here when I bought this mountain," Eloise said. "Previous owner imported stonemasons from Azerbaijan. They took three years to complete. Beautiful work that most people never even notice."\
"I notice everything," Sarah replied sharply. "Including when I'm being manipulated."\
Eloise finally turned, her sharp features unreadable in the morning light. "Please be specific, my dear. I do so hate ambiguity."\
"Project Kebab." Sarah stepped closer, pregnancy-enhanced intuition making her acutely aware of every microexpression. "You didn't bring me here to run research. You wanted someone you could control to weaponize the Gyges Disc technology."\
A smile played at Eloise's lips. "And here I thought you understood the realities of power. Those discs represent the most significant technological advancement since nuclear fission. Did you really believe we'd use them to send interdimensional greeting cards?"\
"You used me," Sarah said flatly. "Married me to Alexander to keep me bound to your family's interests. Put me in charge of the project because you knew I'd be too invested\'97too competent\'97to fail."\
"I provided you with protection when you needed it most." Eloise's voice carried edge now. "Resources, security, purpose. Your son will be born into privilege because of my support."\
"My son?" Sarah's hand instinctively moved to her swollen belly. "Or my insurance policy? Another Shakti heir to guarantee my cooperation?"\
Eloise's laugh was sharp as breaking glass. "You give yourself too much credit. The child is significant because of what he represents\'97advancement beyond humanity's current limitations. Your feelings about Alexander are irrelevant to that fact."\
"Advancement?" Sarah's voice dripped venom. "You mean the panacea virus that's spreading unchecked because your precious son-in-law can't keep his health benefits confined to his wife?"\
Eloise's composure cracked momentarily\'97genuine surprise flickered across her features. "Explain."\
"You didn't know?" Sarah almost laughed. "Your intelligence network failed you? Tony Chantara\'97the Great Teacher himself\'97acquired the virus. He's been sharing its benefits enthusiastically with his devoted followers. Your perfectly controlled outbreak isn't quite so controlled anymore."\
"That's... unfortunate." Eloise turned back to the view, fingers drumming a staccato rhythm on the balustrade. "But not catastrophic. We can manage\'97"\
"Can we? Because last night, in the vortex, I saw something we can't manage. I saw mirrors\'97a dozen universes where variations of us are conducting these same experiments. And in several..." she paused, ensuring Eloise's full attention, "several of those universes, everything was dead."\
Eloise faced her sharply. "Define everything."\
"Cities empty. Technology still functioning but no operators. As if entire populations simply ceased to exist." Sarah stepped closer. "That's what happens when you weaponize forces you don't understand. You don't just kill your enemies\'97you risk killing everything."\
"And yet," Eloise countered, "those who hesitate to act are often the first casualties of those who don't."\
"Is that what this is about? Fear?" Sarah's laugh carried bitter understanding. "You're terrified someone else will develop this technology first. Use it against us."\
"I'm realistic," Eloise corrected. "We live in a world where power determines survival. The Gyges Discs offer power unprecedented in human history. Would you prefer I leave them for others to discover? To weaponize first?"\
"I'd prefer we understand them before we use them." Sarah's voice softened strategically. "Eloise, what happens when your temporal weapons age the wrong population? When reality itself becomes unstable? Your wealth, your power\'97they won't matter in a universe that's unraveling."\
For a moment, silence stretched between them, broken only by the whisper of wind through mountain pines.\
"What are you proposing?" Eloise asked finally.\
"Slow down. Focus on understanding before application. The visions I'm having\'97they're warnings, not invitations." Sarah played her strongest card. "If my son truly represents evolutionary advancement, perhaps that's humanity's real advantage. Understanding these forces organically, not rushing to militarize them."\
Eloise studied her appraisingly. "You've changed, Sarah. Pregnancy has made you... formidable."\
"Desperation has that effect." Sarah gathered her courage. "I'm assuming control of Project Kebab's direction. Full scientific protocols, comprehensive safety testing, and immediate suspension of any weapons development."\
"And if I refuse?"\
Sarah's smile matched Eloise's for sharpness. "Then you'll explain to your board of directors\'97half of whom, I'm told, are now Elvi investors\'97why your daughter-in-law and unborn heir feel unsafe at Sublime Point. Public confidence in Shakti Enterprises' stability might suffer."\
The threat hung crystalline in the alpine air. For the first time, Sarah saw something like respect\'97or perhaps recognition\'97in Eloise's eyes.\
"Seven days," Eloise conceded. "Implement your protocols. Prove me wrong about the urgency. But if another nation, another corporation, demonstrates Gyges weapon capability in that time..."\
"We'll reassess," Sarah finished. "But no more secret tests. No more pushing boundaries without full disclosure."\
Eloise nodded slowly. "The carvings on this balustrade tell a story. A warrior queen who consulted oracles but still wielded her sword. Sometimes the bravest choice is knowing when not to strike."\
"And sometimes," Sarah added, "it's knowing what you're striking at."\
As she walked away, Sarah felt the weight of victory tinged with uncertainty. She'd won a battle, but the war over humanity's future had only just begun. And somewhere in her womb, a child who might hold the key to that future stirred, as if acknowledging the gravity of what his mother had just undertaken.\
